WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:05.290
Since at least 213 BCE, book burnings have
been a reaction to the power of the written

00:00:05.290 --> 00:00:06.290
word.

00:00:06.290 --> 00:00:12.139
When roasting paper in a giant circle went
out of style (at least in the intellectual

00:00:12.139 --> 00:00:17.579
sphere), governments would take it upon itself
to ban books.

00:00:17.579 --> 00:00:23.720
However, when we talk about book bannings
today, at least in the Western World,&nbsp; we

00:00:23.720 --> 00:00:28.929
are usually discussing a specific choice made
by individual schools, school districts, and

00:00:28.929 --> 00:00:34.310
libraries made in response to the moralistic
outrage of some group.

00:00:34.310 --> 00:00:41.010
This, while still hotly-contested and controversial,
is still nothing in comparison to the ways

00:00:41.010 --> 00:00:47.910
books have been removed, censored, and outright
destroyed in the past.

00:00:47.910 --> 00:00:49.850
Along with their authors.

00:00:49.850 --> 00:00:56.510
So on that happy note, let’s … explore
how the seemingly innocuous book has survived

00:00:56.510 --> 00:01:00.380
centuries of the ban hammer.

00:01:07.380 --> 00:01:14.820
Historically speaking there are usually three
big reasons books are banned: Religion, morality,

00:01:14.820 --> 00:01:18.560
and politics.

00:01:18.560 --> 00:01:25.520
Back in the BCE era, entire ideas, religions,
and political movements were wiped out.

00:01:25.520 --> 00:01:31.770
Not with a mass tweet, but with book burnings
and sometimes, if things were really intense,

00:01:31.770 --> 00:01:35.789
people burnings as well.

00:01:35.789 --> 00:01:40.509
One of the earliest documented examples in
North America is the burning of Aztec and

00:01:40.509 --> 00:01:46.909
Mayan manuscripts in the 1560s by Catholic
priests and the Conquistadors.

00:01:46.909 --> 00:01:51.450
In Tudor England during the schism between
the Church of England and the Roman Catholic

00:01:51.450 --> 00:01:58.590
Church, depending on which monarch was arbitrarily
in power and which religion they followed

00:01:58.590 --> 00:02:04.270
your choice of religious book could get you
murdered.

00:02:04.270 --> 00:02:10.550
This was done, as French writer and historian
Lucien X. Polastron explains in “Books on

00:02:10.550 --> 00:02:11.643
Fire: The Destruction of Libraries Throughout
History:”

00:02:11.643 --> 00:02:16.680
“Because, as the lawmakers of ancient China
and the Nazis in Czechoslovakia decided, an

00:02:16.680 --> 00:02:23.080
educated people cannot be governed; because
the conquered peoples must change the history

00:02:23.080 --> 00:02:29.800
of their beliefs, like the Aztecs; because
only the illiterate can save the world, a

00:02:29.800 --> 00:02:36.440
common theme of the millenarian preachers
of every era; because the nature of a great

00:02:36.440 --> 00:02:41.220
collection of books is a threat to the new
power.”

00:02:41.220 --> 00:02:46.090
Historians point to Harriet Beecher Stowe’s
Uncle Tom’s Cabin as the first book in the

00:02:46.090 --> 00:02:51.590
United States to experience a ban on a national
scale.

00:02:51.590 --> 00:02:57.120
Many Southern states prohibited the sale of
the book stating that it stoked the flames

00:02:57.120 --> 00:03:02.460
abolition and Uncle Tom’s Cabin is often
considered the book that helped start the

00:03:02.460 --> 00:03:04.620
Civil War.

00:03:04.620 --> 00:03:09.520
Which … is a hot take, but whatever.

00:03:09.520 --> 00:03:15.100
One place you could call the Billboard Hot
100 of Banned books is the Index Librorum

00:03:15.100 --> 00:03:18.780
Prohibitorum, or List of Prohibited Books.

00:03:18.780 --> 00:03:24.820
This was a list of books and other publications
deemed heretical by the Vatican.

00:03:24.820 --> 00:03:32.370
If you were Catholic you could not read this
content without permission from a priest or

00:03:32.370 --> 00:03:33.370
higher.

00:03:33.370 --> 00:03:34.370
(prayer hands)

00:03:34.370 --> 00:03:40.820
This included romantic texts, astronomy, science,
and anything else the Church saw as antithetic

00:03:40.820 --> 00:03:41.820
to their faith.

00:03:41.820 --> 00:03:46.320
Victor Hugo--Over it
Copernicus and Galileo -- Cancelled

00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:48.620
Rousseau -- ya gotta go

00:03:48.620 --> 00:03:53.320
Funny enough Charles Darwin’s ​On the
​Origin of Species was not listed among

00:03:53.320 --> 00:03:56.150
the banned texts in question.

00:03:56.150 --> 00:04:00.230
Things that make you go hmmm.

00:04:00.230 --> 00:04:06.070
It was banned from the library of Trinity
College, Cambridge and in the United States,

00:04:06.070 --> 00:04:08.400
kept out of many schools.

00:04:08.400 --> 00:04:16.040
In Tennessee the book and teaching of evolution
were banned until as late as 1967.

00:04:16.040 --> 00:04:24.289
In Nazi Germany the government burned thousands
of books written by Jewish authors, “communists”,

00:04:24.289 --> 00:04:26.550
and “others”.

00:04:26.550 --> 00:04:32.419
Included were the works of Albert Einstein,
Sigmund Freud, Ernest Hemingway, Helen Keller,

00:04:32.419 --> 00:04:39.039
Lenin, Jack London, Karl Marx, Upton Sinclair,
Stalin, and Leon Trotsky

00:04:39.039 --> 00:04:44.080
Those are political and religious reasons,
but sometimes a book can get in trouble for

00:04:44.080 --> 00:04:47.509
being too … risque.

00:04:47.509 --> 00:04:52.520
Oscar Wilde’s The Picture of Dorian Gray
is, according to Literary Professor Nicholas

00:04:52.520 --> 00:04:56.780
Frankel, “one of the first novels in the
English language to explore the nature of

00:04:56.780 --> 00:05:00.060
homoerotic and homosexual desire.”

00:05:00.060 --> 00:05:02.370
Charles Whibley, mentee of W.E.

00:05:02.370 --> 00:05:07.449
Henley--the English poet, editor and critic,
who fun fact was also the inspiration for

00:05:07.449 --> 00:05:11.050
Long John Silver--reviewed Dorian as such:

00:05:11.050 --> 00:05:16.940
“[...]Mr Oscar Wilde has again been writing
stuff that were better unwritten; and while

00:05:16.940 --> 00:05:22.470
The Picture of Dorian Gray, which he contributes
to Lippincott’s, is ingenious, interesting,

00:05:22.470 --> 00:05:29.409
full of cleverness, and plainly the work of
a man of letters, it is false art—for its

00:05:29.409 --> 00:05:35.939
interest is medico-legal; it is false to human
nature—for its hero is a devil, it is false

00:05:35.939 --> 00:05:42.210
to morality—for it is not made sufficiently
clear that the writer does not prefer a course

00:05:42.210 --> 00:05:47.759
of unnatural iniquity to a life of cleanliness,
health and sanity.

00:05:47.759 --> 00:05:48.759
[...]

00:05:48.759 --> 00:05:52.580
Mr. Wilde has brains, and art, and style;
but if he can write for none but outlawed

00:05:52.580 --> 00:05:59.210
noblemen and perverted telegraph-boys, the
sooner he takes to tailoring (or some other

00:05:59.210 --> 00:06:04.340
decent trade) the better for his own reputation
and the public morals.”

00:06:04.340 --> 00:06:11.029
–The Scots Observer, July 5th, 1890

00:06:11.029 --> 00:06:16.779
And this is the response to the edited version
that Wilde’s publishers made, where, according

00:06:16.779 --> 00:06:22.830
to Frankel they cut out 500 words from the
original typescript without Wilde’s knowledge.

00:06:22.830 --> 00:06:30.810
When Wilde was put on trial for his homosexuality--“gross
indecency” officially-- in 1895, his novel

00:06:30.810 --> 00:06:33.689
was used as evidence that lead to his conviction.

00:06:33.689 --> 00:06:37.759
This prudishness didn’t just exist for the
posh british victorians.

00:06:37.759 --> 00:06:43.620
We in America had Anthony Comstock, an “anti-vice
activist.”

00:06:43.620 --> 00:06:45.550
He sounds … fun

00:06:45.550 --> 00:06:51.860
Comstock got Congress to pass “The Comstock
Law”&nbsp; on March 3, 1873, which prohibited

00:06:51.860 --> 00:06:53.969
the mailing of “pornographic” materials.

00:06:53.969 --> 00:06:58.069
His definition of the term was murky at best.

00:06:58.069 --> 00:07:05.800
Anatomy textbooks, doctors’ pamphlets dealing
with reproduction, Oscar Wilde, and even The

00:07:05.800 --> 00:07:10.590
Canterbury Tales were deemed too sexy to send
through the mail.

00:07:10.590 --> 00:07:15.150
A bunch of “comstockery” I say.

00:07:15.150 --> 00:07:22.320
Amy Werbel, Professor of the History of Art
at the Fashion Institute of Technology, put

00:07:22.320 --> 00:07:23.529
it aptly:

00:07:23.529 --> 00:07:29.840
“Comstock most typically engaged in “direct
censorship” when he seized and destroyed

00:07:29.840 --> 00:07:33.639
materials to prevent their public circulation.

00:07:33.639 --> 00:07:38.979
Direct censorship in any society, no matter
how repressive, is just one means through

00:07:38.979 --> 00:07:41.569
which discourse is limited.

00:07:41.569 --> 00:07:45.889
Concern about the possibility of censorship
in many cases results in an unwillingness

00:07:45.889 --> 00:07:53.729
of artists and writers to risk producing potentially
indictable work, resulting in self-censorship.

00:07:53.729 --> 00:07:59.430
As individuals respond to perceived limits
in this manner, a new “canon” is formed,

00:07:59.430 --> 00:08:05.509
shaping the perception of what speech is considered
to be normal or transgressive.

00:08:05.509 --> 00:08:10.820
This process of “social censorship” typically
serves to amplify the voices of those who

00:08:10.820 --> 00:08:17.529
already hold power and to reduce, marginalize,
and silence the expressions of those who are

00:08:17.529 --> 00:08:18.529
vulnerable.”

00:08:18.529 --> 00:08:22.370
Two legal cases that really helped&nbsp; move
the conversation in a different direction

00:08:22.370 --> 00:08:30.069
were The United States v. One Book Called
Ulysses &nbsp;in 1933 and R v Penguin Books in

00:08:30.069 --> 00:08:31.069
1960.

00:08:31.069 --> 00:08:37.640
The United States v. One Book Called Ulysses
centered around Irish author James Joyce’s

00:08:37.640 --> 00:08:41.200
Ulysses: a book that I absolutely respect
and hold in high esteem

00:08:42.390 --> 00:08:47.500
The court argued that the book was obscene
and Random House argued that the content was

00:08:47.500 --> 00:08:49.210
protected by the first amendment.

00:08:49.210 --> 00:08:54.060
Ultimately judge John M. Woolsey stated that
the novel was “serious” and that Joyce

00:08:54.060 --> 00:08:59.130
was “sincere and honest” in using a stream
of consciousness to explore the mentality

00:08:59.130 --> 00:09:00.570
of its characters.

00:09:00.570 --> 00:09:05.690
Subsequently the book was finally allowed
to enter the United States as a result.

00:09:05.690 --> 00:09:12.330
In&nbsp; R v Penguin Books Ltd the book in question
was D.H. Lawrence's Lady Chatterley's Lover

00:09:12.330 --> 00:09:17.520
which is a book about a noble woman who has
an affair with her gardener.

00:09:17.520 --> 00:09:19.050
Landscaping can be hot I guess.

00:09:19.050 --> 00:09:24.320
During the trial Mervyn Griffith-Jones, the
prosecutor, asked the jury to decide if the

00:09:24.320 --> 00:09:27.410
book was obscene and if it had enough “literary
merit”

00:09:27.410 --> 00:09:33.760
"Would you approve of your young sons, young
daughters—because girls can read as well

00:09:33.760 --> 00:09:35.790
as boys—reading this book?

00:09:35.790 --> 00:09:40.300
Is it a book you would have lying around your
own house?

00:09:40.300 --> 00:09:46.690
Is it a book that you would even wish your
wife or your servants to read?"”

00:09:46.690 --> 00:09:55.600
Wives and servants … reading … about pleasure
...I GASP

00:09:55.600 --> 00:10:01.080
Aldous Huxley, in a handwritten deposition
to the defence team: "Lady Chatterley's Lover

00:10:01.080 --> 00:10:06.370
is an essentially wholesome book ... That
a beautiful and serious work of art should

00:10:06.370 --> 00:10:12.790
run the risk of being banned because its creator
... chose to make use of certain words that

00:10:12.790 --> 00:10:18.960
it is conventional to regard it as shocking
– this is surely the height of absurdity."

00:10:18.960 --> 00:10:24.430
It seemed like the jury agreed with Huxley
and they ultimately voted that the book was

00:10:24.430 --> 00:10:25.440
not obscene.

00:10:25.440 --> 00:10:33.150
Finally in 1973 with Miller v. California
the US Supreme Court finally tweaked its definition

00:10:33.150 --> 00:10:39.660
of obscenity from that of "utterly without
socially redeeming value" to that which lacks

00:10:39.660 --> 00:10:45.540
"serious literary, artistic, political, or
scientific value."

00:10:45.540 --> 00:10:48.270
Progress?

00:10:48.270 --> 00:10:53.620
Today LGBTQ books and books written by authors
of color depicting the complexities of their

00:10:53.620 --> 00:10:56.780
life are still often challenged.

00:10:56.780 --> 00:11:03.230
Banned Books Week was started in 1982 in response
to a sudden surge in the number of challenges

00:11:03.230 --> 00:11:07.700
to books in schools, bookstores and libraries.

00:11:07.700 --> 00:11:12.910
Additionally, every year, the American Library
Association puts out their list of the Top

00:11:12.910 --> 00:11:14.860
Ten Most Challenged Books.

00:11:14.860 --> 00:11:20.330
In 2019, eight out of the 10 had to do with
LGBTQIA content.

00:11:20.330 --> 00:11:23.880
As Judith Krug, one of the founders of Banned
Books week put it in 1972:

00:11:23.880 --> 00:11:29.490
“... librarians are trained as librarians;
we have absolutely no training or expertise

00:11:29.490 --> 00:11:31.420
in being censors.

00:11:31.420 --> 00:11:38.510
Our job--and yours--is not to limit the horizons
of a child, be he six or twelve, ten or twenty.

00:11:38.510 --> 00:11:44.140
Our jobs are to provide all the ammunition
we possibly can to enable a child to expand

00:11:44.140 --> 00:11:45.900
his horizons.

00:11:45.900 --> 00:11:51.690
The right of any individual to read is an
absolute necessity in a democratic society.”

00:11:51.690 --> 00:11:56.450
Book banning is not about morality it is about
control.

00:11:56.450 --> 00:12:02.340
Controlling the scope of the imagination and
smothering it before it can truly grow.

00:12:02.340 --> 00:12:09.360
It is fine to expose children to complicated
books, but doing so asks us to talk to children,

00:12:09.360 --> 00:12:10.720
not just lecture them.

00:12:10.720 --> 00:12:15.840
We limit the scope of the imagination if we
start policing the quality of a book based

00:12:15.840 --> 00:12:19.510
on subjective moral ideologies.

00:12:19.510 --> 00:12:24.730
Should we be conscious of the kind of work
we expose to children?

00:12:24.730 --> 00:12:30.890
Of course, but banning books because they
include LGBTQ families or because they talk

00:12:30.890 --> 00:12:36.320
candidly about the realities of marginalized existence, ignores that there are children in those

00:12:36.320 --> 00:12:40.320
families and in those realities who need these
books.

00:12:40.320 --> 00:12:45.470
To quote from Krug again, “...‘Intellectual
freedom” is the right of any person to believe

00:12:45.470 --> 00:12:52.790
what he wants on any subject and to express
his beliefs orally or graphically, publicly

00:12:52.790 --> 00:12:55.490
or privately, as he deems appropriate.

00:12:55.490 --> 00:13:01.570
The ability to express one’s opinions, however…
does not really mean very much if there is

00:13:01.570 --> 00:13:07.320
not someone to hear what he is saying, to
read what he is writing, or to view what he

00:13:07.320 --> 00:13:09.800
is producing through other methods.

00:13:09.800 --> 00:13:16.070
And so, the definition of intellectual freedom
has a second integral part and that is total

00:13:16.070 --> 00:13:23.140
and complete freedom of access to all information
and ideas regardless of the medium of communication

00:13:23.140 --> 00:13:24.510
used.

00:13:24.510 --> 00:13:29.360
Information and ideas, in turn, give a man
something to think about, to consider, to

00:13:29.360 --> 00:13:33.190
weigh prior to coming to his own opinion and
decisions.

00:13:33.190 --> 00:13:38.350
Once these opinions and decisions are formed
(and sometimes before!), he, too, is free

00:13:38.350 --> 00:13:40.380
to express his beliefs.

00:13:40.380 --> 00:13:42.420
And so we have a circle.

00:13:42.420 --> 00:13:48.130
The circle breaks, however, if either the
ability to produce or access the productions

00:13:48.130 --> 00:13:49.130
is stifled.”

00:13:49.130 --> 00:13:56.890
So in life we have a choice, do we want to
be Team Comstock or Team Wilde?

00:13:56.890 --> 00:14:00.000
Choose wisely.

